CAT = "cpatcpOwnerPlanning"; SEG = "OPL"
Q = [
("A C corporation is projecting next year's tax. It carries forward a $200,000 NOL from 2023 and expects $150,000 of income before the NOL. At the 21% rate, using the NOL next year saves tax of:",
 "$25,200", ["$31,500", "$42,000", "$16,800"],
 "Only 80% of the year's taxable income can be offset by a post-2017 NOL, so $120,000 is used: $120,000 x 21% = $25,200, and $80,000 carries on.", 25200),
("A corporation already sells into State X, which apportions income by a single sales factor, and plans to build a new plant. Locating the plant in State X rather than another state would:",
 "Not change its X apportionment", ["Raise its State X apportionment", "Lower its State X apportionment", "Add a payroll factor for State X"],
 "Under a single sales factor, property and payroll are ignored, so where the plant sits does not move income into or out of State X."),
("A corporation has 60% of its property, 70% of its payroll and 10% of its sales in State Y, which uses an equally weighted three-factor formula. If State Y moved to a single sales factor, its State Y apportionment would change from:",
 "46.67% to 10%", ["10% to 46.67%", "46.67% to 60%", "70% to 10%"],
 "Three factors give (60% + 70% + 10%) / 3 = 46.67%. With sales alone it is 10%, which favors in-state producers selling mostly elsewhere.", 46.67),
("A calendar-year C corporation learns that its tax rate will fall next year. To lower its total tax, it should consider:",
 "Accelerating deductions into this year", ["Pulling next year's income into this year", "Deferring its deductions to next year", "Postponing its estimated tax payments"],
 "Deductions are worth more at this year's higher rate and income is taxed less next year, so accelerate deductions and defer income where the rules allow."),
("A C corporation that is not a large corporation had $80,000 of tax for a full prior year and expects $120,000 this year. Each quarterly estimated payment needed to avoid the penalty is:",
 "$20,000", ["$30,000", "$27,000", "$22,000"],
 "The required annual payment is the lesser of 100% of this year's tax or 100% of the prior year's: $80,000, paid in four installments of $20,000.", 20000),
("A large corporation had $100,000 of prior-year tax and expects $200,000 this year. It bases its first installment on the prior year. To avoid a penalty, its second installment must be:",
 "$75,000", ["$50,000", "$25,000", "$100,000"],
 "A large corporation may base only its first installment on the prior year, $25,000, and must make up the shortfall in the next: $50,000 + $25,000 = $75,000 (section 6655(d)(2)).", 75000),
("A C corporation wants to give its shareholder land with a basis of $40,000 and a value of $100,000. Compared with selling the land and distributing the cash, distributing the land itself:",
 "Gives the same corporate gain", ["Defers the gain until she sells", "Shifts the gain to the shareholder", "Lets it deduct the land's value"],
 "Section 311(b) treats a distribution of appreciated property as a sale at value, so the corporation recognizes the same $60,000 either way."),
("A shareholder plans to transfer property worth less than its basis to her controlled C corporation in a section 351 exchange. The built-in loss rules may:",
 "Limit the corporation's basis to value", ["Let her recognize the loss on the transfer", "Give the corporation a double loss", "Make the whole exchange fully taxable"],
 "Section 362(e)(2) limits the corporation's basis to value when total basis transferred exceeds value, unless the shareholder elects to reduce her stock basis instead."),
("An S corporation converted from C status 3 years ago. It could sell Asset A, owned at conversion with built-in gain, or Asset B, bought after conversion with the same gain. To avoid built-in gains tax, it should sell:",
 "Asset B, bought after conversion", ["Asset A, held since before conversion", "Either one, as both are taxed alike", "Asset A, before the year ends"],
 "Only gain built in at conversion and recognized during the recognition period is taxed under section 1374. Gain on property bought later passes through without corporate tax."),
("An S corporation plans to issue shares to a corporate investor. The likely result is that:",
 "Its S election terminates", ["The investor's share is taxed at 21%", "Only that investor pays tax", "Nothing changes under 100 owners"],
 "Corporations are not eligible S shareholders (section 1361(b)(1)(B)), so the issuance ends the election from that day."),
("An S shareholder's basis in her loan to the corporation has been reduced by losses. To avoid recognizing gain when the loan is repaid, she should consider:",
 "Delaying repayment until basis returns", ["Taking repayment early, before more losses", "Converting the note to an open account", "Taking repayment in installments this year"],
 "Later net income restores reduced debt basis, so repayment after basis is rebuilt produces no gain. Repayment while basis is low creates gain."),
("A shareholder-employee who works full time for her profitable S corporation takes no salary, only distributions. The main tax risk is that the IRS:",
 "Recasts some of it as wages", ["Revokes the S election", "Taxes the payouts at 21%", "Disallows the corporation's losses"],
 "Distributions avoid employment taxes, but an owner who performs services must receive reasonable compensation, and the IRS can treat part of the distributions as wages."),
("A partner contributes land worth $30,000 with a basis of $45,000. If the partnership later sells it for $30,000, the $15,000 loss is:",
 "Allocated only to that partner", ["Shared by all of the partners", "Disallowed for everyone", "Allocated to the other partners"],
 "Under section 704(c)(1)(C), built-in loss on contributed property is taken into account only by the contributing partner."),
("An owner contributes appreciated land to a partnership rather than selling it and contributing the cash. The usual effect is that the gain is:",
 "Deferred, then allocated to her", ["Avoided because her basis steps up", "Deferred, then shared by all partners", "Taxed to her now, on contribution"],
 "Section 721 defers the gain, and section 704(c) allocates the precontribution gain back to her when the partnership sells the land."),
("Compared with a $40,000 guaranteed payment, a $40,000 nonliquidating cash distribution to a partner whose outside basis is $60,000:",
 "Is not taxable and cuts basis", ["Is ordinary income to her", "Is deductible by the partnership", "Is a $40,000 capital gain"],
 "A guaranteed payment is ordinary income to the partner and deductible by the partnership. A distribution within basis is tax-free and reduces basis to $20,000."),
("When a partner sells her partnership interest, gain attributable to the partnership's unrealized receivables and inventory is:",
 "Taxed as ordinary income", ["Taxed as capital gain", "Taxed as section 1231 gain", "Deferred until it collects"],
 "Section 751(a) treats the amount realized for hot assets as from the sale of non-capital assets, so that part of the gain is ordinary."),
]
